      "text": "To determine the integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7\\) has at least one integer root, we can use the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any rational root of the polynomial \\(P(x)\\) must be a factor of the constant term (7) divided by a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible integer roots are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7\\).\n\nLet's check each of these possible roots:\n\n1. **Root \\(x = -1\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(-1) = (-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + a(-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n   \\]\n   For \\(-1\\) to be a root, \\(P(-1) = 0\\), so we set:\n   \\[\n   9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\(a = 9\\) is a possible value.\n\n2. **Root \\(x = 7\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(7) = 7^3 + 3(7^2) + a(7) + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\(7\\) to be a root, \\(P(7) = 0\\), so we set:\n   \\[\n   497 + 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -497 \\implies a = -65\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\(a = -65\\) is a possible value.\n\n3. **Root \\(x = -7\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(-7) = (-7)^3 + 3(-7)^2 + a(-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -199 - 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\(-7\\) to be a root, \\(P(-7) = 0\\), so we set:\n   \\[\n   -199 - 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -199 \\implies a = -\\frac{199}{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\) must be an integer, \\(a = -\\frac{199}{7}\\) is not a valid solution.\n\nAfter checking all possible integer roots, the only possible values of \\(a\\) that satisfy the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) having at least one integer root are \\(a = 9\\) and \\(a = -65\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{9, -65}\\).",
