      "text": "To find all integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) has at least one integer root, we can use the fact that if \\(r\\) is an integer root of the polynomial, then \\(r\\) must divide the constant term, which is 7. Therefore, the possible integer roots are the divisors of 7, which are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7\\).\n\nWe will test each of these potential roots to see if they satisfy the polynomial.\n\n1. Testing \\(r = 1\\):\n\\[1^3 + 3(1^2) + a(1) + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow a = -11\\]\nSo, \\(a = -11\\) is a possible value.\n\n2. Testing \\(r = -1\\):\n\\[-1^3 + 3(-1^2) + a(-1) + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow a = 9\\]\nSo, \\(a = 9\\) is a possible value.\n\n3. Testing \\(r = 7\\):\n\\[7^3 + 3(7^2) + a(7) + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow 7a = -500 \\Rightarrow a = -\\frac{500}{7}\\]\nSince \\(7a\\) is an integer, \\(a\\) is not an integer in this case.\n\n4. Testing \\(r = -7\\):\n\\[-7^3 + 3(-7^2) + a(-7) + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow -343 - 147 - 7a + 7 = 0 \\Rightarrow -7a = 483 \\Rightarrow a = -69\\]\nSo, \\(a = -69\\) is a possible value.\n\nThus, the possible integer values for \\(a\\) are \\(-11, 9, -69\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -69}\n\\]",
